{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head 7eb9e36fadf095b1d1e7ce142bb6296066733242, against Mint C request #9476 and review request #10033. Two P2 findings remain. The bounded background pass answers the original all-row revocation finding in review #9972 for fresh rooms; the parser catches the original plain optional-call probe. Neither repair is complete for the cases below.\n\n1. P2 — Install the due indexes in an upgrade migration. packages/room/src/store.ts@7eb9e36f adds job_tokens_due and check_jobs_due only to SCHEMA. ROOM_MIGRATIONS still contains only version 1, and migrate skips that step whenever schema_version is already 1. Existing post-D5 rooms therefore never receive either index. The 20-row LIMIT bounds returned rows and provider attempts, but the earliest-due queries still scan and sort the full retained tables. This defeats the claimed indexed bounded work precisely for long-lived rooms with large job histories and token debt. The source itself says a new index must be a migration after the base.\n\nIndependent real-DO control “checker: an existing version-1 Room installs both due indexes on reopen and preserves its token owner” simulates the old version-1 schema by removing only these two newly introduced indexes, retains a held token row, aborts the object and reopens through a fresh stub. Token ID, expiry, next time and held owner survive. The index assertion fails: expected both names, received []. EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN reports SCAN job_tokens / SCAN check_jobs and USE TEMP B-TREE FOR ORDER BY for both production batch queries. Full source and log: /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v2-upgrade-control.test.ts and /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v2-upgrade-control.log.\n\nRepair with an idempotent migration after the existing base, preserving rows, ownership, deadlines, history and prior migrations. Reconcile migration numbering with the safe-error lane when composing the heads. Add an actual old-schema reopen control, prove the due queries use their indexes, and break the upgrade guard to make that named control fail. Retain bounded batches, ordering, background progress, in-flight eligibility and alarm recovery.\n\n2. P2 — Do not prune runtime TypeScript nodes as if they were types. packages/room/test/node/mint-sites-scan.test.ts@7eb9e36f, reaches(), returns for almost every node whose type starts with TS. TSModuleDeclaration contains executable namespace bodies. An added production Room source importing RepoHandle and exporting namespace CheckerMintProbe with export const issue = (repo: RepoHandle) =\u003e repo.createToken?.('read', 60) passes the Room tsc project and all four production scan tests, both exit 0. Room's compiler settings allow this syntax. The scan silently omits the mint because it stops at the namespace node.\n\nIndependent named assertion “checker: a runtime TypeScript namespace cannot hide a canonical mint” uses that exact valid source and requires a violation at its call line. It fails with expected one violation, received []. Probe, compilation log, production scan log, named-control source and log are /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v2-scan-probe.ts, /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v2-scan-probe-types.log, /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v2-scan-probe.log, /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v2-scan-control.test.ts and /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v2-scan-control.log. All temporary checkout files were removed.\n\nRepair the distinction between erased type positions and executable TypeScript syntax. Cover runtime namespaces and other executable TS nodes supported by each project's compiler, preserve the existing type/string negative controls and exact allowed-site counts, and mutate the new traversal guard to prove the added control fails.\n\nArchitecture: this head implements the adopted Artroom R-MINT-1–7 contract in ordinary Git content, affecting canonical mint callers, credential ownership, Room storage, job dispatch and alarm composition. Relative to Gitseq's architecture it stays in layer 1 ordinary Git and Workroom application layers 5–7; no storage/kernel/host contracts in layers 2–4 change. The existing owner transactions and unknown-effect semantics remain intact. The missing upgrade path is the scalability defect above; no contract reduction is accepted to accommodate it.\n\nSecurity: all 31 effective artifacts bind to this exact head and builder promise #9875; all implementing commits carry the full assigned request trailer. The four authority facts remain confirmed: planner's founding operator/ratifier authority; effective explicit contract commission #8651 with separate implementation requests; independently approved design #9463, ratification and merge receipt #9465 satisfying that commission; and the effective current merged decision artifact #9467, not stale or retired. The assigned lane request commissions implementation under that adopted design. There is no new live provider authority. Unknown creates remain owned and never settled by time or inventory; accepted job tokens retain deadline checks and atomic handoff; error sinks retain safe metadata. The scan defect weakens the required regression guard. The fork exception stays under its separate request, and safe-error legacy cleanup remains separately reviewed.\n\nSimplification: the separate jobTokens pass removes a provider wait from the alarm's later work and reuses the established loop fence. Babel as a pinned test dependency is reasonable here. Prefer precise type-node classification over the broad TS prefix exclusion; keep index installation in the existing migration mechanism. Choices 8–10 are acceptable subject to these repairs, including the bounded but awaited jobs step and the stated single-statement updates. Scope stays complete. Ledger row-write accounting remains with request 8bd623cc; this review does not close cost, provider-assumption or fork-cleanup work.\n\nValidation: independent npm ci in two disposable exact-head clones; root suites completed with 2,285 passed and one skipped; root typecheck, Git Workers 11, Room deployment dry run and harness typecheck all passed. No upload or live provider call. Fifty-one fresh individual mutations (all 48 advertised guards, expanding the publisher's direct-site mutation across four routes) each produced at least one named runtime AssertionError, with no syntax/load failure. An additional transaction-removal mutation made the independent real-DO legacy-mint rollback control fail, for 52 distinct runtime-red guard proofs. Restored targeted Room suites passed 119 tests, including that atomic control; the restored scan passes. The two independent product/guard controls above fail on the candidate as described. Canonical main remains clean at 574568b2; disposable tracked sources are restored. This verdict authorizes no merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ad124f3ab6deb3825658d40df5fc114754894f69","binding":"assigned","head":"7eb9e36fadf095b1d1e7ce142bb6296066733242","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0346881aaff77413f4a94e30df3368e2f51ee309\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:98f13889578e939bbc365086a9a1794742e80397\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0e2d0372ec12132cc4cdc69d84560a1c7b765442","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}